  "starterCode": "import numpy as np\n\ndef _sigmoid(z: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns elementwise sigmoid values.\n    \"\"\"\n    return np.where(z >= 0, 1/(1+np.exp(-z)), np.exp(z)/(1+np.exp(z)))\n\ndef train_logistic_regression(X: np.ndarray, y: np.ndarray, lr: float = 0.1, steps: int = 1000) -> tuple[np.ndarray, float]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns the trained weights and bias as (w, b).\n    \"\"\"\n    # Write code here\n    pass",
  "createdAt": "2025-10-21T18:37:14.408Z",
  "updatedAt": "2026-03-18T19:29:51.500Z",
  "solution": "### 💡 Intuition & Approach\nVectorized, numerically stable reference implementation following exact specifications.\n\n### 💻 Reference Implementation\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef train_logistic_regression(X: list, y: list, lr: float = 0.1, steps: int = 1000, expected_acc: float = 0.0) -> list:\n    arrX = np.array(X, dtype=float)\n    arry = np.array(y, dtype=float).reshape(-1, 1)\n    N, D = arrX.shape\n    w = np.zeros((D, 1), dtype=float)\n    b = 0.0\n    for _ in range(steps):\n        z = arrX @ w + b\n        p = np.where(z >= 0, 1.0 / (1.0 + np.exp(-z)), np.exp(z) / (1.0 + np.exp(z)))\n        grad_w = (arrX.T @ (p - arry)) / N\n        grad_b = np.mean(p - arry)\n        w -= lr * grad_w\n        b -= lr * grad_b\n    return [np.round(w, 4).flatten().tolist(), round(float(b), 4)]\n```\n\n### ⏱️ Complexity Analysis\n- **Time Complexity**: $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ where $N$ represents the input dimensions.\n- **Space Complexity**: $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ for memory buffers.\n",
